---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
const example = `services:
  web:
    image: nginx
    ports:
      - "8080:80"
  ollama:
    image: ollama/ollama
    ports:
      - "127.0.0.1:11434:11434"
  portainer:
    image: portainer/portainer-ce
    ports:
      - "9000:9000"
---
services:
  other-app:
    ports:
      - "8080:3000"`;
---
<Base image="/og/tool-compose-port-checker.png" title="Docker Compose port checker" description="Paste one or more docker-compose files to find duplicate host ports and services exposed to your whole network. Runs in your browser; nothing is uploaded.">
  <div class="prose" data-pagefind-body>
    <h1>Docker Compose port checker</h1>
    <p class="lede">Paste your compose files to find <strong>port conflicts</strong> between stacks and services that are <strong>open to your whole network</strong> when they probably shouldn't be.</p>
    <p class="meta">Runs entirely in your browser. Nothing you paste is uploaded or stored.</p>
  </div>

  <form class="tool" id="pc">
    <fieldset>
      <legend>Compose files (separate several files with a line containing only <code>---</code>)</legend>
      <textarea id="input" spellcheck="false" aria-label="docker-compose content">{example}</textarea>
      <p style="margin:10px 0 0"><button type="submit" class="action" id="run">Check ports</button></p>
    </fieldset>
  </form>

  <div id="results" aria-live="polite"></div>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>How to read the result</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Whole network</strong> (<code>"8080:80"</code> or <code>0.0.0.0</code>): every device that can reach this machine can connect. Fine for things meant to be used from other devices, like a media server.</li>
      <li><strong>This machine only</strong> (<code>"127.0.0.1:8080:80"</code>): only the host itself and other containers can reach it. Best for APIs and databases that only other containers use.</li>
      <li><strong>Conflict</strong>: two services want the same host port and protocol. The second one fails to start with "port is already allocated".</li>
      <li>Ports published by Docker <strong>bypass ufw</strong>, so binding to <code>127.0.0.1</code> is the reliable way to keep something private. Background: <a href="/blog/homelab-security-no-port-forwards/">My homelab has zero open ports</a>.</li>
      <li>Not checked: ports from <code>.env</code> variables like <code>${'${PORT}'}:80</code> (listed as "couldn't read"), and <code>network_mode: host</code> services, which use the host's ports directly and are listed separately.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <script is:inline src="/tools/port-checker.js"></script>
  <script is:inline>
    (function () {
      var esc = function (s) { return String(s).replace(/[&<>"']/g, function (c) { return { '&': '&amp;', '<': '&lt;', '>': '&gt;', '"': '&quot;', "'": '&#39;' }[c]; }); };
      var label = { all: '<span class="warn">Whole network</span>', local: '<span class="ok">This machine only</span>', specific: 'Specific IP' };
      function portText(p) { return p.from === p.to ? p.from : p.from + '–' + p.to; }
      function run() {
        var pc = window.PortChecker;
        var r = pc.parse(document.getElementById('input').value);
        var c = pc.conflicts(r.ports), s = pc.sensitive(r.ports);
        var h = '';
        h += '<h2>Conflicts</h2>' + (c.length
          ? '<ul>' + c.map(function (x) { return '<li class="warn">' + esc(x.a.service) + ' (file ' + x.a.file + ') and ' + esc(x.b.service) + ' (file ' + x.b.file + ') both use ' + portText(x) + '/' + esc(x.a.proto) + '</li>'; }).join('') + '</ul>'
          : '<p class="ok">No port conflicts found.</p>');
        h += '<h2>Worth a second look</h2>' + (s.length
          ? '<ul>' + s.map(function (x) { return '<li><strong>' + esc(x.port.service) + '</strong> port ' + x.number + ' is open to the whole network: ' + esc(x.why) + '. Consider <code>127.0.0.1:' + x.number + ':…</code> and reaching it through a reverse proxy or VPN.</li>'; }).join('') + '</ul>'
          : '<p class="ok">No well-known admin or database ports exposed to the whole network.</p>');
        if (r.hostNet.length) h += '<p><strong>Host networking:</strong> ' + r.hostNet.map(function (x) { return esc(x.service); }).join(', ') + ' (uses the host\'s ports directly, not shown below).</p>';
        if (r.unparsed.length) h += '<p><strong>Couldn\'t read:</strong> ' + r.unparsed.map(function (x) { return '<code>' + esc(x.entry) + '</code> (' + esc(x.service) + ')'; }).join(', ') + '</p>';
        h += '<h2>All published ports (' + r.ports.length + ')</h2>';
        if (r.ports.length) {
          var rows = r.ports.slice().sort(function (a, b) { return a.from - b.from; }).map(function (p) {
            return '<tr><td>' + portText(p) + '/' + esc(p.proto) + '</td><td>' + esc(p.service) + '</td><td>' + p.file + '</td><td>' + (p.ip ? esc(p.ip) : '(all)') + '</td><td>' + label[pc.exposure(p.ip)] + '</td></tr>';
          }).join('');
          h += '<table class="result-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Host port</th><th scope="col">Service</th><th scope="col">File</th><th scope="col">Bound to</th><th scope="col">Reachable from</th></tr></thead><tbody>' + rows + '</tbody></table>';
        } else h += '<p>No published ports found. Did you paste a file with a <code>services:</code> section?</p>';
        document.getElementById('results').innerHTML = h;
      }
      document.getElementById('pc').addEventListener('submit', function (e) { e.preventDefault(); run(); });
      run();
    })();
  </script>
</Base>
